review_notes:
I found no justified simp-interface problem in this file. I read all 65 lines:
- the private `mapBiprod_square` and its `simp only`/`rw` proof;
- the local biproduct instances;
- the `completedOddDoubleImageIso` definition;
- the `completed_oddDouble_corner_square` theorem.

I also searched the project for every use of `completedOddDoubleImageIso` and `completed_oddDouble_corner_square`. The only matches are inside this file. I compared it with the parallel file `SourceOddDoubleEvaluation.lean`, which has `oddDoubleImageIso`, `oddDouble_corner_square` and `BiprodEvaluationSquare.square`.

The file declares no `@[simp]`, `@[reassoc]` or `@[simps]` attributes, so there are no rules to compete, loop or rewrite in the wrong direction. The proof of `mapBiprod_square` uses only standard rewrite lemmas in their usual direction (`Iso.trans_hom`, `Functor.mapBiprod_hom`, `biprod.mapIso_hom`, `biprod.map_fst/_snd` and their `_assoc` forms, `biprod.lift_fst_assoc/_snd_assoc`). The backward `rw [← Functor.map_comp]` is a local step that folds two maps back into one before rewriting. It is not a sign that some simp lemma points the wrong way.

I considered three possible additions and rejected each:
- `@[simps hom]` on `completedOddDoubleImageIso`;
- `@[reassoc]` on `completed_oddDouble_corner_square`;
- a simp lemma giving the projections of the iso's hom.

None of the declarations has a downstream user, so there is no actual use that would benefit. The parallel source-side file does not use these attributes either. Recommending them would mean adding annotations only because they look natural, which the rubric excludes.

There is one observation outside this rubric. The private `mapBiprod_square` looks like a duplicate of `BiprodEvaluationSquare.square`, which the sibling file uses. That is about duplication, not the simp interface, so I did not report it.
